  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Commercial throughput in the Strait of Hormuz continued its stratified pattern through the first week of October: IMF PortWatch recorded approximately 3 commercial transits per day on a 7-day rolling average as of Oct 1, while Windward's more generous AIS count placed individual daily readings at 13 vessels on Oct 4. The barrel-volume recovery tracked by Kpler reached approximately 80% of pre-war levels — a figure driven primarily by Iranian authorization of very large crude carriers under the IRGC selective-passage toll system — while vessel count remained at 15–35% of pre-crisis norms. UKMTO issued at least one confirmed attack advisory per day through the week ending Oct 7, and the Strait narrows remain hostile to routine commercial transit absent a security guarantee. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F11</span> without move</p>

      <p>The divergence between barrel-volume and vessel-count metrics demonstrates that Iranian control of the passage is deliberately calibrated to maximize revenue while sustaining strategic pressure: high-value VLCC cargoes are licensed at approximately $2M per transit while routine commercial traffic remains suppressed by daily IRGC fast-boat and limpet-mine activity. Ghalibaf (Oct 4) publicly stated seven conditions must be met before Hormuz reopens to normal transit — conditions the United States has not accepted and that Iran has sequenced opposite to US demands. Windward's 13-vessel Oct 4 reading and the Sep 26-28 peak of approximately 20-24 vessels remain the high-water marks for the crisis period; neither approached the 30-vessel/day threshold that the now-resolved F4 set as its resolution bar. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>On the diplomatic and kinetic track, Vance (Oct 7) demanded nuclear enrichment reductions incompatible with Iranian public positions, and Pezeshkian characterized US negotiations as "meaningless." The Pentagon, per Axios (~Oct 3), has directed CENTCOM to complete strike preparations, with no execution date and no final presidential authorization in place. This preparation order is the most significant near-term escalation indicator on the board and drives <span class="cite">F12</span> entry at 38%. Trump has withheld authorization in at least two comparable prior windows; the 23-day October window will test whether the rhetorical "easy way or hard way" framing converts to kinetic action or reverts to the established pattern of preparation without execution. A ceasefire deal (<span class="cite">F9</span>, p=17% by Nov 5) remains theoretically available — the UNGA sidelines session Sep 22-23 confirmed the channel is open — but current public positions on both sides leave no overlap. → moved <span class="cite">F12</span> (new, 38%), supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>

      <p>The Israeli election campaign enters its final 19 days with Yashar (Eisenkot) holding a consistent but narrow polling lead over Likud (Netanyahu). Four of five major polls sampled Oct 5–8 show Yashar at 22–23 seats against Likud at 18–21, with only i24NEWS as a Likud-ahead outlier. Polymarket prices Yashar at approximately 62% for winning the most seats. Neither bloc approaches a governing majority in any current poll, which means the election outcome determines who forms a government but not whether a stable government can form quickly. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span> (42%→36%)</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:38%;">Escalation 38%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:45%;">Protracted 45%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:17%;">De-esc 17%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (38%):</strong> Pentagon preparation orders are confirmed and presidential authorization remains the sole outstanding trigger; Trump's "easy way or hard way" framing with a 23-day window represents the highest-probability acute escalation path on the board (F12, p=0.38).</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (45%):</strong> The dominant outcome remains neither deal nor major US strikes — sustained IRGC Hormuz disruption, near-daily UKMTO advisories, and diplomatic positioning that generates sessions without convergence, with neither side accepting the other's sequencing on reopening conditions.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (17%):</strong> Iran demonstrated willingness to sign under pressure in April and June 2026, the UNGA channel is open, and midterm-adjacent US incentives could shift Trump's calculus — but current public positions on enrichment and Hormuz sequencing leave no overlap (F9, p=0.17 by Nov 5).</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Internal Iranian decision-making on the war's continuation involves at least three distinct institutional axes: the Supreme Leader's office, which holds final authority on nuclear and strategic questions; the IRGC command structure, which controls Hormuz passage policy operationally and profits from the selective-transit toll system; and the elected executive (Pezeshkian/Araghchi), which manages diplomatic communications and whose public positions have been systematically more conciliatory than IRGC-signaled redlines. This three-way structure complicates external analysis: Araghchi's Aug 15 statement that Iran had "not yet made a decision to restart negotiations" was a Foreign Ministry position, not an IRGC position, and the two are not necessarily aligned. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Ghalibaf's Oct 4 formulation — seven preconditions before Hormuz reopens — came from the Majlis speaker, whose institutional alignment is closer to the IRGC than to the Pezeshkian executive. The sequencing of conditions (Hormuz reopening contingent on conditions the US has not accepted) is the inverse of the US sequencing (ceasefire first, then normalization). This structural mismatch has been the operative constraint on deal-making since the June MOU collapsed in July. The UNGA sidelines channel (Sep 22-23) demonstrated that message exchange can continue even during active CENTCOM-IRGC kinetic exchange; it does not demonstrate that either side can move its institutional redlines. → supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>

      <p>The internal Iranian economy is experiencing stratified pressure: oil export revenues are partially recovering via VLCC IRGC toll arrangements, while the rial and domestic consumer prices remain under sanctions-plus-war-risk stress. This dual-track situation does not map onto a simple "Iran is winning or losing" frame — the IRGC toll revenue creates institutional incentives to sustain rather than resolve the Hormuz closure, while the elected government faces domestic inflation pressure that would ease with normalization. Pezeshkian's "meaningless negotiations" characterization may be less a strategic position than a response to IRGC constraint on his negotiating latitude. → supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>
    </div>
  </div>

